			{
				"Overview",
				"International organizations are composed of primarily member states, but may also include other entities, such as other international organizations, commmercial firms, and [[Non-governmental organization|nongovernmental organizations]]. Additionally, entities may hold observer status. Under international law, although treaties are typically between states, intergovernmental organizations also have the capacity to enter into treaties. The traditional view was that only states were subjects of international law, but with the founding of the United Nations, that view expanded to include intergovernmental organizations.",
