		sections = {
			{
				"Overview",
				"The League's primary goals were stated in its eponymous Covenant. They included preventing wars through collective security and disarmament and settling international disputes through negotiation and arbitration. The Covenant of the League of Nations was signed on 28 June 1919 as Part I of the [[Treaty of Versailles]], and it became effective with the rest of the Treaty on 10 January 1920. [[Australia]] was granted the right to participate as an autonomous member nation, marking the start of Australian independence on the global stage. The first meeting of the Council of the League took place on 16 January 1920, and the first meeting of the Assembly of the League took place on 15 November 1920. In 1919, U.S. president Woodrow Wilson won the [[Nobel Peace Prize]] for his role as the leading architect of the League. Despite this, he was ultimately unsuccessful in getting his country to join it.",
